.TH SYS 1 "September 2026" "system-config" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sys \- set up, sync and check this Mac
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sys
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys
is the single entry point to the configuration repository in
.IR ~/.system-config .
It sets up a new machine, keeps an existing one in step with the repository,
saves local changes back, and reports on what is installed.
.PP
The repository is the source of truth. Anything it tracks \(em dotfiles, colour
palettes, Sublime Text settings \(em is mirrored onto the machine by
.BR "sys sync" ,
and mirrored back by
.BR "sys push" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Set up this machine from scratch. Installs Homebrew and everything in the
.IR Brewfile ,
copies dotfiles into place, installs language versions with
.BR mise (1),
restores app licences from the keychain, installs App Store apps and applies
macOS defaults. Finishes by printing the steps that need a human.
.IP
Safe to re-run. Use
.B \-\-licences
or
.B \-\-mas
to run only that part.
.TP
.B sync
Pull the latest configuration and apply it here. Equivalent to the
.B usys
alias.
.TP
.B push
Save this machine's configuration into the repository, commit and push.
Equivalent to the
.B bsys
alias.
.TP
.B extras
Install the optional extras listed in
.I Brewfile.optional
and
.IR config/mas\-apps\-optional.txt .
Never run by
.BR "sys setup" ;
these are large or occasional installs you ask for explicitly.
.TP
.B dock
Rebuild the Dock from
.IR config/dock ,
in order. Applications not installed on this machine are skipped. Setup does this only
if it has not already succeeded on this machine, so a Dock you have since
rearranged is never overwritten; this command forces it.
.PP
Steps like this record that they ran in
.IR .state/ ,
one file per feature, holding a fingerprint of the config they last succeeded
with. The record is written only when the step actually did its work, so a
half finished install picks up where it left off; and editing the config makes
the step run again on the next
.BR "sys setup" .
.B sys push
records the current Dock back to
.IR config/dock .
.TP
.B remove
Undo everything setup installed: Homebrew and its packages, mise toolchains,
the dotfiles it copied, editor configuration and the Dock. Lists what it will remove and asks before doing
anything;
.B \-\-dryrun
shows the list and stops. Afterwards it prints the commands for creating a
fresh admin user with remote login and Screen Sharing, which is how to start
over on a machine with no display attached.
.IP
It does not erase macOS and does not touch your user account, SSH or Screen
Sharing, so a headless machine stays reachable. Erase All Content and Settings
reboots into Setup Assistant, which needs a physical keyboard and display.
.TP
.B alias
List your aliases, functions and project starters, grouped by section, read
straight from
.IR dotfiles/.aliases .
Also available as the
.B la
alias.
.TP
.B mise
Print a short guide to using
.BR mise (1)
for language versions, and
.BR uv (1)
for Python packages.
.TP
.B doctor
Check that the expected tools are installed, report language versions, whether
each licence is in the keychain, whether the App Store is reachable, and whether
the repository has uncommitted changes. Changes nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen without changing anything. Accepted by every command.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
.RB ( setup )
Set the computer, host and local host name. Requires
.BR sudo (8).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-brew
.RB ( setup )
Skip Homebrew and packages.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-macos
.RB ( setup ", " sync )
Skip macOS defaults.
.TP
.B \-\-licences
.RB ( setup )
Only restore licences from the keychain.
.TP
.B \-\-mas
.RB ( setup )
Only install App Store apps.
.TP
.B \-\-dryrun
.RB ( remove )
Show what would be removed and stop. Same as
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.SH HOW SYNCING WORKS
.B sys sync
mirrors three directories:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dotfiles              \(-> ~
colors                \(-> ~/Library/Colors
config/sublime\-config \(-> ~/Library/Application Support/Sublime Text/User
config/cursor         \(-> ~/Library/Application Support/Cursor/User
.fi
.RE
.PP
The contents of each repository directory are the manifest. To track a new file,
put it in the right directory; there is no list to update. To stop tracking one,
delete it.
.PP
The repository always wins. Before replacing a system file whose contents
differ, the old version is copied to
.I ~/.system-config/.drift
and a note is printed, so a local edit you forgot to
.B sys push
is never lost.
.I .drift
is overwritten each run rather than accumulating; git is the history.
.SH EDITORS
VS Code has Settings Sync, so its settings, keybindings and extensions follow
your account and are deliberately not tracked here.
.PP
Cursor does not ship Settings Sync, so its
.I settings.json
and
.I keybindings.json
are mirrored from
.IR config/cursor ,
and its extensions are listed in
.IR config/cursor\-extensions.txt .
.B sys setup
installs any that are missing;
.B sys push
refreshes the list so it cannot drift.
.SH LANGUAGES
.BR mise (1)
owns Ruby, Node and Python. Global versions live in
.IR ~/.config/mise/config.toml ;
override them per project with a local
.IR mise.toml .
After changing a version, run
.B sys push
then
.BR "mise install" .
Python packages and virtual environments go through
.BR uv (1).
.SH LICENCES
Licences are kept in the macOS keychain and never in the repository.
.I config/licences
maps a keychain service name to a destination path, one per line, separated by
a vertical bar. Add a licence with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
openssl base64 \-A \-in License.sublime_license |
  security add\-generic\-password \-U \-a "$USER" \\
    \-s sublime\-text\-license \-w "$(cat)"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The keychain does not follow you to a new Mac, so
.B sys setup
names any missing licence and prints the exact command to add it.
.B sys doctor
reports the same.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.system-config/Brewfile
Applications and command-line tools.
.TP
.I ~/.system-config/macos.sh
macOS defaults.
.TP
.I ~/.system-config/config/licences
Keychain service names mapped to destination paths.
.TP
.I ~/.system-config/config/mas\-apps.txt
App Store application IDs.
.TP
.I ~/.system-config/Brewfile.optional
Optional extras, installed only by
.BR "sys extras" .
.TP
.I ~/.system-config/config/mas\-apps\-optional.txt
Optional App Store apps, installed only by
.BR "sys extras" .
.TP
.I ~/.system-config/dotfiles/
Copied into the home directory.
.TP
.I ~/.system-config/.drift/
System files replaced by the last sync. Not tracked by git.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ROOT_DIR
Location of the repository. Default
.IR ~/.system-config .
.TP
.B DRY_RUN
Set to 1 for the effect of
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.TP
.B ASSUME_YES
Set to 1 for the effect of
.BR \-\-yes .
.SH EXAMPLES
Set up a brand new Mac:
.PP
.RS
.nf
curl \-fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dwkns/\\
system\-install/master/install.sh | bash
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what a sync would change, without changing it:
.PP
.RS
.B sys sync \-\-dry\-run
.RE
.PP
Save a change made directly to a system file:
.PP
.RS
.B sys push
.RE
.PP
Re-run only the licence step after adding one to the keychain:
.PP
.RS
.B sys setup \-\-licences
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mise (1),
.BR uv (1),
.BR brew (1),
.BR mas (1),
.BR defaults (1),
.BR security (1)
.PP
.I ~/.system-config/README.md
